(1) Credit institutions, their shareholders, members of their bodies, employees and any other persons acting for credit institutions must not disclose or exploit secrets that have been entrusted or made accessible to them solely on the basis of business relationships with customers or on the basis of section 75(3) (banking secrecy). The duty of secrecy applies without limit in time. Where officials of authorities and of the Oesterreichische Nationalbank learn, in the course of their official duties, of facts that are subject to banking secrecy, they may publish those facts or make them accessible only if they are not subject to secrecy under section 6 of the Informationsfreiheitsgesetz – IFG, BGBl. I Nr. 5/2024.

(2) The duty to observe banking secrecy does not apply
1. in criminal proceedings, towards the public prosecutors' offices and the courts in accordance with sections 116 and 210(3) of the Strafprozeßordnung 1975 – StPO, BGBl. Nr. 631/1975, and, in criminal proceedings for intentional fiscal offences other than fiscal regulatory offences, towards the fiscal criminal authorities in accordance with sections 89 and 99(6) of the Finanzstrafgesetz – FinStrG, BGBl. Nr. 129/1958;
2. where there is a duty to provide information under section 41(1) and (2), section 61(1), section 93 and section 93a;
3. on the death of the customer, towards the probate court and the court commissioner;
4. where the customer is a minor or is otherwise under guardianship, towards the guardianship court;
5. where the customer expressly consents to the disclosure of the secret; the consent must clearly describe the cases to which it applies and must be given either in writing or electronically by means of a clear affirmative act;
6. for general information on the economic situation of an undertaking that is customary in banking, unless the undertaking expressly objects to the provision of the information;
7. in so far as the disclosure is necessary to clarify legal matters arising from the relationship between the credit institution and the customer;
8. as regards the reporting obligation under section 25(1) of the Erbschafts- und Schenkungssteuergesetz;
9. where there is a duty to provide information to the FMA under the WAG and the BörseG;
10. for the purposes of the automatic exchange of information on financial accounts under the Gemeinsamer Meldestandard-Gesetz – GMSG, BGBl. I Nr. 116/2015, and for the fulfilment of the due diligence and reporting obligations under the Krypto-Meldepflichtgesetz – Krypto-MPfG, BGBl. I Nr. 96/2025;
11. towards the federal tax authorities and the Amt für Betrugsbekämpfung in response to a request for information under section 8 of the Kontenregister- und Konteneinschaugesetz – KontRegG, BGBl. I Nr. 116/2015;
12. as regards the transmission obligation under section 3 of the KontRegG and the provision of information under section 4 of the KontRegG;
13. as regards the reporting obligation under sections 3 and 5 of the Kapitalabfluss-Meldegesetz, BGBl. I Nr. 116/2015;
14. as regards the provision of information under section 16(6) of the FM-GwG and the exchange of information under section 22(2) and section 24(6) of the FM-GwG, in each case for the prevention of money laundering or terrorist financing;
15. as regards the transmission obligation under section 18a(8) of the Umsatzsteuergesetz 1994 – UStG 1994, BGBl. Nr. 663/1994, for the purposes of Article 24b of Regulation (EU) No 904/2010;
16. as regards the provision of information under section 12(2) and (3) of the Sanktionengesetz 2024 – SanktG 2024, BGBl. I Nr. 5/2025, and the exchange of information under section 12(6) and section 14(2) of the SanktG 2024, in each case for monitoring compliance with sanctions measures;
17. for the purposes of fulfilling the reporting obligation and of the automatic exchange of reports under the EU-Meldepflichtgesetz, BGBl. I Nr. 91/2019.

(3) A credit institution may not invoke banking secrecy in so far as the disclosure of the secret is necessary to determine its own tax liability.

(4) Subsections (1) to (3) also apply to financial institutions and insurance undertakings as regards section 75(3), and to protection schemes, except for the cooperation with other protection schemes and with deposit guarantee schemes and investor-compensation schemes required under sections 93 to 93b.

(5) (Constitutional provision) Subsections (1) to (4) may be amended by the Nationalrat only in the presence of at least half of its members and by a majority of two thirds of the votes cast.

(6) Where the use of means of distance communication has been agreed with the customer for the provision of banking transactions, the requirement of written form for the customer's release from banking secrecy under subsection (2), point 5, may, by way of derogation from section 886 of the ABGB, be met by strong customer authentication within the meaning of section 4, point 28 of the ZaDiG 2018.

