(1) Without prejudice to the validity of the legal transaction, credit agreements and any amendments to them must be drawn up on paper or on another durable medium. The creditor must provide all contracting parties with a copy of the credit agreement without delay after its conclusion.

(2) The credit agreement must specify in a clear and concise manner:
1. the type of credit;
2. the identities, addresses, telephone numbers and email addresses of the contracting parties and, where applicable, the identity and address of the credit intermediary involved;
3. the total amount of credit and the conditions governing the drawdown;
4. the duration of the credit agreement;
5. in the case of linked credit agreements, the goods or services concerned and their cash price;
6. the borrowing rate, or all borrowing rates if different borrowing rates apply in different circumstances, the conditions governing the application of each borrowing rate and, where available, any index or reference rate applicable to each initial borrowing rate, as well as the periods, conditions and procedures for changing each borrowing rate;
7. the annual percentage rate of charge and the total amount payable by the consumer, calculated at the time the credit agreement is concluded, stating all the assumptions used in that calculation;
8. the amount, number and frequency of payments to be made by the consumer and, where appropriate, the order in which payments will be allocated to different outstanding balances charged at different borrowing rates for the purposes of repayment;
9. where capital amortisation of a credit agreement with a fixed duration is involved, the right of the consumer to receive, on request and free of charge, at any time throughout the duration of the credit agreement, a statement in the form of an amortisation table (section 22);
10. if charges and interest are to be paid without capital amortisation, a statement showing the periods and conditions for the payment of the borrowing interest and of any associated recurrent and non-recurrent fees;
11. where applicable, the charges for maintaining one or more compulsory accounts recording both payment transactions and the amounts of credit drawn down, the charges for using a means of payment for both payment transactions and withdrawals, any other charges deriving from the credit agreement and the conditions under which those charges may be changed;
12. the rate of interest applicable in the case of late payments as applicable at the time of the conclusion of the credit agreement and the arrangements for its adjustment, and, where applicable, any charges payable for late payment;
13. a warning regarding the consequences of missing or late payments;
14. where applicable, a reference to notarial fees payable;
15. the security and insurance required, if any;
16. the existence or absence of a right of withdrawal and, where applicable, the withdrawal period and other conditions for exercising the right of withdrawal, including the durable medium to be used for the notification under the first sentence of section 25(4), information concerning the obligation of the consumer under the first sentence of section 25(5) to repay the capital drawn down and the interest, and the amount of interest per day;
17. the type of durable medium that the consumer chooses for receiving the following information:
a) where applicable, the reminder under section 10(7);
b) the information referred to in section 23;
c) the information on changes in the borrowing rate under section 24(1);
d) where applicable, the information under section 33 and section 34, and
e) where applicable, the information on the termination of an open-end credit agreement under section 27;
18. where applicable, information concerning the rights arising from section 26 and the conditions for exercising those rights;
19. a reference to the right of early repayment under section 29, the procedure for early repayment and, where applicable, information concerning the creditor's right to compensation, together with a transparent and comprehensible explanation of how the compensation owed by the consumer to the creditor is to be calculated;
20. the procedure to be followed in exercising the right to terminate the credit agreement;
21. the possibility for the consumer of having recourse to an out-of-court complaint or redress procedure and the conditions for that access;
22. where applicable, other contractual terms and conditions;
23. the name and address of the competent supervisory authority;
24. the relevant contact details of providers of debt advisory services and a recommendation to the consumer to contact those providers in the event of repayment difficulties.

(3) The information referred to in subsection (2) must be clearly legible, must take into account the technical constraints of the medium on which it is displayed and must be displayed in an adequate and suitable way on the different channels.

(4) In the case of a loan with a repayment vehicle, the credit agreement must include, in addition to the information under subsection (2), a clear and concise statement that, in particular, neither the credit agreement nor the contract on the repayment vehicle provides a guarantee for the repayment of the total amount drawn down under the credit agreement, unless such a guarantee is expressly given. This does not apply to credit agreements with a total amount of credit of less than 200 euros or to credit agreements under which the credit is to be repaid within three months and only insignificant charges are payable.

(5) The following applies in the event of the defects in the credit agreement listed below:
1. Where the credit agreement does not state the borrowing rate, the annual percentage rate of charge or the total amount payable by the consumer, the interest rate referred to in section 1000(1) of the ABGB is deemed to be the agreed borrowing rate, unless a lower borrowing rate was agreed. In the case of an instalment loan, the creditor must calculate the resulting reduced instalments and inform the consumer of them.
2. Where the annual percentage rate of charge is stated too low in the credit agreement, a borrowing rate is deemed to have been agreed that corresponds to that statement, taking into account the other contents of the contract. The second sentence of point 1 applies accordingly.
3. Where the credit agreement does not state the conditions under which the borrowing rate or other charges may be changed, the creditor may not make such changes to the detriment of the consumer.
4. Where the credit agreement does not state the right of early repayment or the right to compensation, the creditor may not claim compensation.
The legal consequences referred to in points 1 to 4 do not arise where the consumer has already received the information missing or incorrectly stated in the credit agreement, in line with the subsequent contractual arrangements, as part of the pre-contractual information under section 10(1).

