(1) Creditors must, where appropriate, show reasonable forbearance before enforcement proceedings are initiated. Such forbearance measures must take into account, among other things, the individual circumstances of the consumer. Except in justified cases, creditors are not obliged to offer forbearance measures to consumers repeatedly.

(2) The forbearance measures referred to in subsection (1)
1. may include, among other things, a total or partial refinancing of a credit agreement;
2. include a modification of the existing terms and conditions of a credit agreement, which may include, among other things:
a) an extension of the duration of the credit agreement;
b) a change of the type of credit agreement;
c) a deferral of payment of all or part of the instalment repayments for a period;
d) a reduction of the borrowing rate;
e) an offer of a payment holiday;
f) partial repayments;
g) currency conversions;
h) partial forgiveness and a debt consolidation.

(3) Where the terms and conditions of the credit agreement are modified under subsection (2), point 2, the obligation to carry out a creditworthiness assessment under section 17 does not apply, provided that the total amount payable by the consumer is not significantly increased by the modification of the credit agreement.

(4) Creditors must refer consumers who are experiencing difficulties in meeting their financial commitments to debt advisory services that are easily accessible to the consumer.
